// The tag class the `intent` word widens to has to be unnameable, or
// `fn h() -> Intent` smuggles an intent out of a function and past every
// restriction above.
//
// docs/specs/M31.md proposed `$Intent`, on the ground that the lexer never
// forms an identifier containing `$`. True -- but it does form `$Intent` as ONE
// hole token whose LEXEME is exactly "$Intent", and the host resolves a type by
// lexeme, so `-> $Intent` was accepted and handed back the tag. The class is
// therefore called `$Intent$`: a hole is `$` followed by identifier characters
// and `$` is not one, so no single token can ever spell it.
// expect-error: tests/13-tag-unnameable.lx:14: type expected
#include "../lib/prelude.lx"

fn h() -> $Intent { return 0; }

fn main() -> i64 { return 0; }
